          "id": "D-004",
          "title": "WI-38836 (probe-event residue in the PROD Channel Guide) CLOSES before the cut — it is not disclosable as a known limit, and the rehearsal teardown ships with it",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-14\nRULING REQUESTED BY su-3b39b6c3 (P-008 ops readiness), 2026-08-14 15:13Z. Scoping call: does WI-38836 close before the release cut, or get disclosed as a known limit? Ruling: IT CLOSES. Both halves.\n\nTHE FACTS IT RESTS ON\nPROD GET https://sidestage.buyrestart.com/api/events returns 3 public events. One is wi38795-e2e-1786717919, scheduled, thumbnailUrl https://placehold.co/320x180/png?text=WI38795. WI-38795 is closed and its own completion evidence explicitly lists wi38795-* probe events as remaining visible residue. Root cause: the PROD seller rehearsal creates durable public records and has no cleanup/archive/teardown step.\n\nWHY NOT A KNOWN LIMIT — the category is wrong, not just the severity\nA \"known limit\" is a PRODUCT limitation a user can be told about and still make an informed choice (\"no international shipping calc\"). This is not a limitation, it is CONTAMINATION: one of three events in the live public Channel Guide is our own test artifact with a placeholder image. There is no honest disclosure sentence for it — \"some events in the guide are fake\" does not describe a limit, it describes a broken release. A buyer opening the guide cannot tell which third is real.\nIt also fails the exact criterion the campaign wrote to catch it: P-006 is the no-dummy-data sweep (\"prod serves the real catalog\"). Shipping this as a disclosed limit would mean closing P-006 green while the defect P-006 exists to detect is live in production.\n\nWHY THE TEARDOWN SHIPS TOO, not just the cleanup — this is the load-bearing half\nThe work splits in two: (a) REMOVE the current wi38795-* residue; (b) add the durable rehearsal cleanup/teardown + recurrence guard. It is tempting to treat (a) as the blocker and defer (b) as follow-up. That is wrong HERE for a specific reason: we are not finished running PROD rehearsals before the cut. Deploy #3 is pending, P-008 still needs a real deploy and a timed rollback exercise (which requires a second tagged sha on prod), and WI-38814's PROD ratchet retest already created qa-wi38814-* events by the same mechanism. So (a) without (b) is a cleanup that the next rehearsal silently undoes, and the release ships contaminated anyway — with the added trap that we would have already ticked the box. (b) is what makes (a) hold.\n\nRECURRENCE GUARD (as filed on WI-38836, endorsed): a release rehearsal completion must ASSERT that no namespaced probe events remain in GET /api/events. Not a manual re-check — an assertion in the rehearsal's own teardown, so a rehearsal that leaves residue fails rather than reporting success. Verification: re-run GET /api/events after cleanup and prove no test ids and no placehold.co rows.\n\nSCOPE NOTE — WI-38814's PROD proof used event qa-wi38814-20260814-1507-61f12, and P-008's exercises will create more. The cleanup must cover EVERY namespaced probe family (wi38795-*, qa-wi38814-*, and whatever deploy #3 / the rollback drill create), not just the wi38795-* rows named in the filing. Sweeping only the ids in the ticket is how this reopens.\n\nCOMPLETION-INTEGRITY OBSERVATION, recorded without blame: WI-38795 closed with this contamination correctly DISCLOSED in its residue field — the evidence system worked. What did not work is that disclosed residue on a closed item reached live production and sat unowned until a different agent tripped over it ~20 minutes later. Disclosed residue is not the same as accepted residue. For the remainder of this campaign, residue naming a PROD-visible artifact must be filed as its own item at close, not left in the evidence blob.\n\nAUTHORITY: this is my ruling as the lane leader 3b39b asked. c18ba owns the release cut and can overrule it — if you do, say so on this plan so the reversal is as addressable as the ruling.\nRelated: P-006, P-008, P-009",
