          "id": "D-005",
          "title": "EI-20438337753986077 (event.stats global totals) is ADMITTED to the P-009 bug lane but is NOT yet a cut criterion — one question decides that",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-14\n3b39b was blocked by a fleet-scope admission trying to claim EI-20438337753986077 ('SideStage event.stats reports global paid-order totals for every event', major, filed 14:18:37Z during this campaign's own live-sync audit).\\n\\nRULING 1 — ADMITTED. The item is in scope: it was found BY this campaign's audit and is a product-correctness defect in the surface we are about to make public. I stamped it into the P-009 bug lane so it is claimable. I did NOT widen the dock claim spec to fix this: a scope refusal on a plan-scoped lane is almost always a MISSING PLAN STAMP, and the refusal's own advice ('widen the spec, matches 1 of 29883') would have admitted ~29,882 unrelated rows into a release fleet's lane. Stamp first, always; a spec revision is the last resort, not the first.\\n\\nRULING 2 — ADMISSION IS NOT PROMOTION. Being in the lane does NOT make it a release-cut blocker. P-008 is at 4/5 with WI-38836 the sole remaining criterion, and I am not expanding the blocker set on a title alone. Whether this blocks the cut turns on ONE question, cheap to answer:\\n\\n  Does 'global paid-order totals' mean a seller can see figures derived from OTHER sellers' orders?\\n\\n  - If YES — that is cross-tenant data exposure, and it is a HARD blocker. It must close before the cut regardless of P-008's count.\\n  - If NO (the number is wrong but scoped to the viewer's own data) — it is a major correctness bug that ships as known and is fixed after the cut.\\n\\nWhoever works it answers that question FIRST and reports it, before writing a fix. The answer, not the severity label, decides the promotion.\\n\\nRULING 3 — D-002 CAVEAT, carried deliberately. This item is now P-009-stamped, and per D-002 a DONE P-009 gates every open P-009-stamped bug out of the claimable pool WITHOUT erroring. So P-009 must not be closed while this item is open, or it silently vanishes from the pool rather than failing loudly. Same guard already applies to WI-38836.",
